WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000 align:start position:0% line:0%
Transcriber: Leslie Gauthier

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.072
I’m a bald man --

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:07.139
(Laughter)

00:00:07.139 --> 00:00:08.541
but I don’t miss my hair.

00:00:09.558 --> 00:00:12.545
What I do miss
is going to the hairdresser.

00:00:13.596 --> 00:00:17.783
I found it to be such an enjoyable
and relaxing experience.

00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:21.420
I loved it when they would
rub their fingers through my hair

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.222
while they washed
my hair in the sink.

00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:26.158
I enjoyed it when
they walked me over to the chair,

00:00:26.158 --> 00:00:27.927
and they would play with my hair more

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:31.113
and talk to me about different hairstyles
and haircuts I could get.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:35.051
And I even enjoyed
the gentle sound of the scissors --

00:00:35.051 --> 00:00:37.336
snip, snip, snip --

00:00:37.336 --> 00:00:38.504
right next to my ears.

00:00:39.171 --> 00:00:42.558
I enjoyed it so much
and I was so deeply relaxed

00:00:42.558 --> 00:00:44.860
that I remember many times
I would just sit there

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:49.498
and I’d think, “I’d pay you even
if you just pretended to cut my hair.”

00:00:49.498 --> 00:00:50.733
(Laughter)

00:00:51.834 --> 00:00:53.135
There’s another moment also

00:00:53.135 --> 00:00:55.554
that often stimulates
this deep relaxation for me.

00:00:56.005 --> 00:00:57.590
It’s whenever I get an eye exam.

00:00:57.857 --> 00:00:58.858
(Laughing)

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:02.561
And I’m not talking about those puffs
of air they blow into your eyeballs.

00:01:02.878 --> 00:01:05.364
That just makes my eyelids flutter
thinking about it.

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:10.419
But instead it’s when
they bring out the wheels of lenses.

00:01:10.419 --> 00:01:11.420
(Laughter)

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.255
And they click through them all.

00:01:13.255 --> 00:01:16.625
And they want to know
which lens helps you see better.

00:01:17.426 --> 00:01:19.095
“One or two?”

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:20.146
(Laughter)

00:01:20.429 --> 00:01:22.264
“One or two?”

00:01:22.364 --> 00:01:23.365
(Laughter)

00:01:23.682 --> 00:01:26.719
And they say it
in this very relaxing voice.

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:27.720
(Laughter)

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.890
And there’s something about
that moment of personal attention

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:34.376
and that soft voice
that just deeply relaxes me.

00:01:34.527 --> 00:01:38.697
And it gives me these light,
sparkling, pleasurable brain tingles.

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:42.101
I enjoy it so much

00:01:42.101 --> 00:01:45.271
that as they click through each option,

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:47.106
I want it to last a little bit longer.

00:01:47.106 --> 00:01:50.009
So when they ask me, “One or two?”

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:51.827
I say, “Oh, I’m not sure.”

00:01:51.827 --> 00:01:53.913
(Laughter)

00:01:55.331 --> 00:01:59.535
Another moment that often stimulates
this deep form of relaxation

00:01:59.535 --> 00:02:03.839
is when I watch the TV show,
Bob Ross’s “Joy of Painting.”

00:02:03.839 --> 00:02:06.942
(Laughter and applause)

00:02:07.209 --> 00:02:09.028
I would come home from school,

00:02:09.028 --> 00:02:10.563
throw some pillows on the floor,

00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:11.564
lie down

00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:14.099
and let that soft voice
just sweep over me.

00:02:15.484 --> 00:02:17.953
And he had the nicest personality.

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:20.206
He’d turn to the camera,

00:02:20.206 --> 00:02:21.257
he’d look right at me,

00:02:21.257 --> 00:02:25.060
and he’d say the kindest things
like,  “There are no mistakes,

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:26.512
just happy accidents.”

00:02:26.512 --> 00:02:27.513
(Laughter)

00:02:27.513 --> 00:02:29.565
And then he’d turn back to his canvas

00:02:29.565 --> 00:02:32.768
and he’d make these gentle,
relaxing sounds with his paintbrush.

00:02:33.402 --> 00:02:34.970
Tap, tap ...

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:36.071
tap, tap, tap.

00:02:36.639 --> 00:02:40.226
And I found it so relaxing
that I often fell asleep

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:42.061
and never saw him finish the painting.

00:02:42.178 --> 00:02:43.179
(Laughter)

00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:49.702
In 2013, I learned that this special
response has a special name.

00:02:50.519 --> 00:02:54.723
Autonomous Sensory Meridian Response,

00:02:54.723 --> 00:02:57.293
or much more simply, ASMR.

00:02:59.044 --> 00:03:01.730
That is a complicated word,

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:03.949
so it’s a good thing
we’ve got it simplified.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:06.018
Now, I’m a physiologist.

00:03:06.402 --> 00:03:07.519
I’m a researcher.

00:03:08.037 --> 00:03:09.138
I’m a data nerd.

00:03:09.405 --> 00:03:12.758
I was so excited to read
about the science of ASMR.

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:18.647
In 2013, there wasn’t a single
peer-reviewed research study.

00:03:19.598 --> 00:03:22.751
What I did find were plenty of forums:

00:03:22.751 --> 00:03:24.670
thousands and thousands of comments,

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:26.005
all across the internet,

00:03:26.005 --> 00:03:27.940
discussing this blissful sensation.

00:03:28.824 --> 00:03:32.027
And they all reported
the same response that I felt.

00:03:32.561 --> 00:03:34.263
They were deeply relaxed,

00:03:34.263 --> 00:03:35.581
they felt comforted,

00:03:35.581 --> 00:03:36.999
they felt calmed

00:03:36.999 --> 00:03:40.386
and they had these light,
sparkly brain tingles.

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:43.672
And I also noticed

00:03:43.672 --> 00:03:46.091
that every scenario they described

00:03:46.091 --> 00:03:48.143
that stimulated their ASMR

00:03:48.143 --> 00:03:49.345
had a simple theme to it.

00:03:50.045 --> 00:03:54.550
It was always when they were receiving
positive personal attention

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:56.335
from a kind and caring individual.

00:03:57.937 --> 00:04:01.640
And the examples they gave
were very similar to my moments.

00:04:02.341 --> 00:04:04.576
It was moments with hairdressers;

00:04:04.576 --> 00:04:06.996
it was moments
with health care professionals;

00:04:06.996 --> 00:04:08.814
it was moments with teachers.

00:04:09.265 --> 00:04:13.502
They’d tell stories of the teacher
from their childhood with the soft voice

00:04:13.502 --> 00:04:15.187
who would kneel down next to them

00:04:15.187 --> 00:04:17.139
and help them solve a math problem.

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:19.391
It was moments with parents.

00:04:20.109 --> 00:04:21.944
It was moments with their best friends

00:04:21.944 --> 00:04:24.413
when they would whisper
to each other in childhood.

00:04:24.413 --> 00:04:26.749
Or they’d braid each other’s hair.

00:04:26.749 --> 00:04:28.584
Or they’d play that game

00:04:28.584 --> 00:04:32.788
where you just lightly with your finger
draw letters on each other’s backs

00:04:32.788 --> 00:04:34.273
and try to guess the letter.

00:04:35.157 --> 00:04:38.327
But the moment that stimulated ASMR

00:04:38.327 --> 00:04:40.629
that I saw the most often mentioned

00:04:40.629 --> 00:04:44.850
was watching the TV show, 
Bob Ross’s “Joy of Painting.”

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:46.335
(Laughter)

00:04:46.885 --> 00:04:51.240
Now we know Bob Ross was not
trying to stimulate people’s ASMR

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:52.941
or cause them to relax.

00:04:53.325 --> 00:04:55.327
He was trying to teach you how to paint.

00:04:55.778 --> 00:05:01.300
But there are individuals today
who are creating ASMR videos

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:04.236
just for the purpose
of relaxing their viewers

00:05:04.236 --> 00:05:06.305
and helping them
to fall asleep more easily.

00:05:06.855 --> 00:05:09.758
They’re called ASMR artists,

00:05:09.758 --> 00:05:12.244
and they have millions of followers.

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:17.299
Some of them have more followers
to their YouTube channel

00:05:17.299 --> 00:05:20.602
than Kim Kardashian has
to her YouTuve channel.

00:05:21.754 --> 00:05:23.706
And what are they doing in these videos?

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:26.408
Well very simply,

00:05:26.408 --> 00:05:28.610
they’re sitting close to the camera,

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:30.829
they’re gazing into the lens,

00:05:30.829 --> 00:05:32.414
and they’re speaking softly

00:05:32.414 --> 00:05:33.882
or they're whispering gently.

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:38.237
They may even be pretending
to be a hairdresser

00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:40.956
or pretending to be
a health care professional

00:05:40.956 --> 00:05:44.326
or just speaking softly
the way a close friend would to you.

00:05:45.928 --> 00:05:49.498
And right now ...

00:05:49.498 --> 00:05:51.433
I’d like to introduce you

00:05:51.433 --> 00:05:54.403
to some very popular
ASMR artists on YouTube.

00:05:55.437 --> 00:05:56.872
So lean back,

00:05:56.872 --> 00:05:58.040
stretch out your legs.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:05:59.041
(Laughter)

00:05:59.041 --> 00:06:00.109
And enjoy.

00:06:02.327 --> 00:06:03.479
(Video) ASMR Glow: Good.

00:06:11.737 --> 00:06:12.738
Good.

00:06:12.738 --> 00:06:13.739
One last time.

00:06:19.661 --> 00:06:20.662
Great.

00:06:22.898 --> 00:06:26.301
(Video) WhispersRed ASMR: So there
are lots of different things we need.

00:06:26.418 --> 00:06:30.672
Some we would have
in our regular spa kit.

00:06:31.173 --> 00:06:33.792
Some of them are quite obvious.

00:06:34.076 --> 00:06:35.077
(Breathing)

00:06:35.727 --> 00:06:38.313
Things like ...

00:06:38.313 --> 00:06:40.032
cotton buds ...

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:42.384
and ...

00:06:42.384 --> 00:06:43.735
cotton pads.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:48.607
(Video) Paris ASMR: I’ve brought
some tasty tea from Paris.

00:06:50.259 --> 00:06:51.777
For you, yeah.

00:06:53.712 --> 00:06:58.901
It is called, “Weekend à Paris.”

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:02.521
“Weekend in Paris.”

00:07:06.091 --> 00:07:09.878
I think it takes like, um ...

00:07:09.878 --> 00:07:11.513
um, rose.

00:07:17.736 --> 00:07:19.655
(Video) Gentle Whispering ASMR: Alright.

00:07:19.655 --> 00:07:20.839
A bit to the front here.

00:07:21.924 --> 00:07:22.925
(Scissors cut)

00:07:26.094 --> 00:07:27.095
(Scissors cut)

00:07:27.296 --> 00:07:28.297
OK.

00:07:29.998 --> 00:07:31.366
Stay still.

00:07:31.366 --> 00:07:32.367
(Scissors cut)

00:07:33.252 --> 00:07:34.253
(Scissors cut)

00:07:36.638 --> 00:07:38.490
Stay to make sure ...

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:40.726
(Scissors cut)

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:43.662
It’s nice and even.

00:07:45.414 --> 00:07:46.415
Mhm.

00:07:50.769 --> 00:07:53.005
Craig Richard: Now,
if you felt deeply relaxed

00:07:53.005 --> 00:07:56.358
or maybe you felt
those special brain tingles,

00:07:56.358 --> 00:08:00.496
you might have been wondering,
“What is going on inside my brain?”

00:08:01.346 --> 00:08:02.931
Well, we wondered the same thing.

00:08:03.465 --> 00:08:07.002
I recently published
a brain scan study with Bryson Lochte

00:08:07.002 --> 00:08:09.655
and other researchers
from Dartmouth College.

00:08:10.439 --> 00:08:13.709
And we saw that the brain regions
that are activated

00:08:13.709 --> 00:08:17.312
while people are watching
ASMR videos like these

00:08:17.312 --> 00:08:20.082
are the same brain regions
that are activated

00:08:20.082 --> 00:08:23.485
when people are receiving
positive personal attention

00:08:23.485 --> 00:08:26.221
from a kind and caring person
in the real world.

00:08:27.472 --> 00:08:31.143
I’ve also collected data
from over 30,000 participants

00:08:31.143 --> 00:08:34.696
as part of a research study
with Karissa Burnett and Jennifer Allen.

00:08:35.631 --> 00:08:39.167
And Jennifer Allen is the woman
who coined the term

00:08:39.167 --> 00:08:42.521
Autonomous Sensory
Meridian Response in 2010.

00:08:43.772 --> 00:08:47.960
And what we see
is that ASMR is experienced

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:50.429
in over 130 different countries.

00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:53.966
And the people are reporting
the same experience.

00:08:54.199 --> 00:08:55.734
They feel deeply relaxed.

00:08:55.934 --> 00:08:58.837
They have these special,
light brain tingles.

00:08:59.571 --> 00:09:02.541
And it’s helping them
to fall asleep more easily,

00:09:02.541 --> 00:09:04.643
and it’s helping them
to reduce their stress.

00:09:05.561 --> 00:09:11.149
Even individuals who are diagnosed
with anxiety and insomnia

00:09:11.149 --> 00:09:14.453
are reporting benefits
from watching these ASMR videos

00:09:14.453 --> 00:09:16.138
and experiencing ASMR.

00:09:17.472 --> 00:09:21.043
Other institutions
are also publishing data about ASMR

00:09:21.043 --> 00:09:23.445
and they’re reporting
similar health benefits.

00:09:24.129 --> 00:09:25.280
One study in particular,

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:28.584
from the University of Sheffield 
in the United Kingdom,

00:09:28.584 --> 00:09:32.054
showed that when people watch ASMR videos,

00:09:32.054 --> 00:09:36.058
their heart rates significantly
and effectively are reduced.

00:09:37.242 --> 00:09:39.628
Now there’s plenty
of unanswered questions still.

00:09:40.128 --> 00:09:44.850
Like, what might be the brain chemicals
that are causing this amazing sensation?

00:09:46.068 --> 00:09:50.505
One likely candidate
is the neural hormone oxytocin,

00:09:50.522 --> 00:09:53.191
and you might have heard
of this as “the love hormone.”

00:09:54.176 --> 00:09:55.811
It’s already well known

00:09:55.811 --> 00:09:59.681
that your oxytocin levels
in your brain increase

00:09:59.681 --> 00:10:02.851
when anyone gives you
positive personal attention

00:10:02.851 --> 00:10:04.369
in a kind and caring way.

00:10:04.936 --> 00:10:09.224
It's also already known that your oxytocin
levels, when they increase,

00:10:09.224 --> 00:10:10.726
you’ll feel relaxed,

00:10:10.726 --> 00:10:12.010
you’ll feel calmed,

00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:13.095
you’ll feel comforted.

00:10:14.096 --> 00:10:18.150
Another big question is why doesn’t
everyone experience ASMR?

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:21.336
You might have been watching
those video clips and been like,

00:10:21.336 --> 00:10:23.905
“I didn’t feel any of those
amazing brain tingles.

00:10:23.905 --> 00:10:24.906
Grrr!”

00:10:25.207 --> 00:10:26.208
(Laughter)

00:10:27.242 --> 00:10:30.962
Well, it could be that those clips
aren’t the ones that work for you.

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:33.799
If you've never felt this experience,

00:10:33.799 --> 00:10:36.802
maybe you just haven’t had
the right real-world encounter yet.

00:10:37.736 --> 00:10:39.488
Or maybe it's determined at birth.

00:10:40.305 --> 00:10:45.994
It could be determined by the sequence
of your genes related to oxytocin

00:10:45.994 --> 00:10:47.546
or other brain chemicals.

00:10:49.631 --> 00:10:54.936
Another big question is how
do these health benefits of ASMR compare

00:10:54.936 --> 00:10:58.974
to the widely reported
health benefits of yoga,

00:10:58.974 --> 00:11:00.292
of mindfulness,

00:11:00.292 --> 00:11:01.426
of meditation?

00:11:01.877 --> 00:11:07.499
And how do these health benefits of ASMR
compare to the therapeutic pros and cons

00:11:07.499 --> 00:11:10.585
of medications used
to treat anxiety and insomnia?

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:14.039
And lastly,

00:11:14.039 --> 00:11:17.759
will health professionals
someday advocate ASMR

00:11:17.759 --> 00:11:20.128
as another tool in the toolbox

00:11:20.128 --> 00:11:23.448
to help people manage their stress
and their sleeplessness?

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:28.904
I don’t have the answer
to those questions ...

00:11:28.904 --> 00:11:30.105
but I do know one thing.

00:11:32.941 --> 00:11:36.862
I know that my vision of a blissful heaven

00:11:36.862 --> 00:11:39.164
is Bob Ross greeting me
at the pearly gates

00:11:39.164 --> 00:11:42.951
and asking me, “Craig, would
you like a haircut and an eye exam?”

00:11:43.118 --> 00:11:44.119
(Laughter)

00:11:44.119 --> 00:11:45.120
(Applause)

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:47.789
(Applause and cheers)

